From: nightcarnage <nightcarnage@bc250-project>
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] drm/amdgpu: disable ThinLTO for amdgpu_ttm.o on BC-250

ThinLTO optimizes away the NULL guard in amdgpu_ttm_tt_unpopulate(), causing
kernel panics (CR2=0x18, NULL->mapping) when a partially-populated BO is freed
after a GPU command failure.

The guard is:

    for (i = 0; i < ttm->num_pages; ++i)
        if (ttm->pages[i])
            ttm->pages[i]->mapping = NULL;

With ThinLTO the compiler proves that pages[i] is never NULL across the entire
call graph and removes the check. Adding -fno-lto to this single compilation
unit defeats the optimization while keeping LTO for the rest of the module.

This works around the underlying GPU aliasing bug (documented in GabriWar's
analysis: the GPU reads outside its own page table) by preventing the cleanup
path from crashing the kernel. The system survives instead of panicking.

Signed-off-by: nightcarnage
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/Makefile | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/Makefile b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/Makefile
index 1234567..89abcde 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/Makefile
@@ -44,6 +44,8 @@ subdir-ccflags-y += -Wno-override-init
 subdir-ccflags-$(CONFIG_DRM_AMDGPU_WERROR) += -Werror
+#
+# BC-250/gfx1013: prevent ThinLTO from eliding the NULL guard in unpopulate
+CFLAGS_amdgpu_ttm.o += -fno-lto
 
 CFLAGS_amdgpu_trace_points.o := -I$(src)
